\f0\fs24 \cf0 The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering, University of Canterbury\
\
Scope: A brief explanation of the Geometric wavefront sensor demonstration code\'85\
\
Purpose: The example code contained in this directory can be used to demonstrate how the geometric wavefront sensor works by using Fresnel diffraction, generating a wavefront, propagating a distorted wavefront between two defocused planes, and then using a wavefront sensor, i.e, the geometric WFS, to determine the phase of the distortion, in terms of Zernike modes.\
\
To run this example of the geometric wavefront sensor, simply place these files and directories in a directory of your choice, make sure you have a copy of Matlab, make sure you are the active directory of Matlab that contains \'93geometric_WFS_main.m\'94, and type \'85\
   \
  
\f1\i geometric_WFS_main
\f0\i0 \
\
in Matlab\'92s command window. \
\
There is no need to use any parameters to run this file. Once run however, you will see some numbers on the command window as Matlab creates the inverse matrix of the wavefront sensor, and then three figures should be show. Figure 1 is an extra-focal image of the wavefront sensor, Figure 2 is the intra-focal image of the wavefront sensor, and Figure 3 is the Zernike terms generated from the geometric WFS. \
\
A few notes about this example may be helpful\'85\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	1.	}No noise has added to this version of the geometric WFS.\
{\listtext	2.	}An inverse matrix is constructed that will provide a means to determine a set of Zernike modes that produces a randomly generated phase screen.\
{\listtext	3.	}The random phase screen uses Kolmogorov statistics and is based on the aperture diameter, D, over the Fried parameter, i.e, the strength of the turbulence, D/r_0.\
{\listtext	4.	}Two defocused planes are generated using Fresnel diffraction. \
{\listtext	5.	}An original planar wavefront, i.e, \'93circle\'94 is propagated through the phase screen \'91new_midpoint(\'85)\'92, and two defocused planes, intra-focal (-z) and extra-focal (+z) are created, and displayed in Figures 2 and 3, respectively.  \
{\listtext	6.	}Lastly, the geometric WFS is called with parameters of i) both defocused image planes, ii) the distance parameter from focus, z, the inverse matrix that was created earlier, and lastly, the number of \'91angles\'92 used in the Radon transform to determine the number of slices required of the 3D wavefront so that it can be processed using \'91angles\'92 (about 8) 2D images.\
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
